    "replace": "The Postgres tests (`PostgresSessionTaintStoreTests`, 4) and the migration's trigger have not been checked because Docker is unavailable locally. CI is the verifier.",
    "type": "telegraphic",
    "reason": "The colon label and dropped verbs make the status unclear; the reason and the CI role should be stated in full sentences."
  }
]

[
  {
    "find": "A vector database is a storage system optimized for indexing and querying high-dimensional embeddings — the numerical representations that machine learning models produce for text, images, or other data.",
    "replace": "A vector database is a storage system optimized for indexing and querying high-dimensional embeddings. These embeddings are numerical representations that machine learning models produce for text, images, or other data.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The sentence runs over 25 words and puts the definition of embeddings in an appositive after a dash, so the reader has to hold two ideas at once."
  },
  {
    "find": "a vector store enables fast similarity search over chunk embeddings",
    "replace": "a vector store lets you quickly search chunk embeddings by similarity",
    "type": "buried-verb",
    "reason": "The action is hidden in 'enables fast similarity search'; the rewrite uses the verb 'search' and names the person doing it."
  },
  {
    "find": "embedding-based retrieval surfaces conceptually related results",
    "replace": "embedding-based retrieval returns conceptually related results",
    "type": "jargon",
    "reason": "'Surfaces' is a spatial metaphor for returning results; 'returns' says what retrieval does."
  },
  {
    "find": "Similarity lookups across millions of items become tractable.",
    "replace": "You can run similarity lookups across millions of items.",
    "type": "buried-verb",
    "reason": "The action is hidden in the noun 'lookups' and the abstract phrase 'become tractable'; the rewrite states who can do what."
  },
  {
    "find": "**When you probably don't:**",
    "replace": "**When you probably don't need one:**",
    "type": "telegraphic",
    "reason": "The heading drops the object 'need one'; the contrast with 'When you need one' has to be inferred."
  },
  {
    "find": "If your dataset is under ~100k vectors, a library like FAISS or even pgvector in your existing Postgres instance will likely suffice, avoiding the operational overhead of a separate service.",
    "replace": "If your dataset is under ~100k vectors, a library like FAISS or even pgvector in your existing Postgres instance will likely suffice. Using one of these avoids the operational overhead of a separate service.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The sentence runs over 25 words and the trailing 'avoiding' clause makes the reader infer who avoids the overhead and how it connects to the main clause."
  },
  {
    "find": "It's worth noting that many",
    "replace": "Many",
    "type": "redundant",
    "reason": "The phrase announces that a point is worth noting instead of simply stating it."
  }
]

[
  {
    "find": "Packet loss, detected via three duplicate ACKs or a retransmission timeout, is interpreted as a congestion signal.",
    "replace": "Packet loss is interpreted as a congestion signal when it is detected via three duplicate ACKs or a retransmission timeout.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The aside about how loss is detected splits the subject from its verb, forcing the reader to hold the sentence open."
  },
  {
    "find": "With fast retransmit/fast recovery, cwnd is halved;",
    "replace": "With fast retransmit and fast recovery, cwnd is halved;",
    "type": "telegraphic",
    "reason": "The slash stands in for 'and' where plain prose should use the word."
  },
  {
    "find": "Modern variants like CUBIC (the Linux default) and BBR diverge significantly: BBR, for instance, models bottleneck bandwidth and RTT directly rather than relying on loss as the primary signal, which yields better throughput on high-latency, lossy links.",
    "replace": "Modern variants such as CUBIC (the Linux default) and BBR differ significantly. BBR, for instance, models bottleneck bandwidth and RTT directly instead of relying on loss as the primary signal. This yields better throughput on high-latency, lossy links.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The sentence is long and packs an example and a result into one clause; 'which' also leaves unclear what yields better throughput."
  }
]

[
  {
    "find": "This change is designed to better align our check-ins with the sprint cadence, giving us",
    "replace": "We are making this change to better align our check-ins with the sprint cadence. This gives us",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The original sentence runs long and leaves the actor implicit; splitting it and naming the team makes the reason easier to follow."
  },
  {
    "find": "more meaningful opportunity",
    "replace": "chance",
    "type": "empty-words",
    "reason": "Meaningful is a vague hype word; chance is plain and keeps the point."
  },
  {
    "find": "The calendar invite has been updated — please accept the new series.",
    "replace": "The calendar invite has been updated, so please accept the new series.",
    "type": "telegraphic",
    "reason": "The dash stands in for a connector; restoring so makes the instruction read as a full sentence."
  },
  {
    "find": "will help streamline our communication and ensure our time together is as productive as possible",
    "replace": "will help make our communication more efficient and help ensure we use our time together as productively as possible",
    "type": "jargon",
    "reason": "Streamline is business jargon; make more efficient says the same thing in plain words."
  }
]

[
  {
    "find": "With over 10 years of experience building distributed systems at scale, Maya has driven initiatives spanning event-driven architecture adoption, multi-region failover, and the migration of a monolithic billing engine to a fleet of 40+ microservices handling 3 million transactions per day.",
    "replace": "Maya has more than 10 years of experience building distributed systems at scale. She has led initiatives to adopt event-driven architecture and enable multi-region failover, and she led the migration of a monolithic billing engine to a fleet of 40+ microservices. That fleet handles 3 million transactions per day.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The original packs her experience, a three-item list of initiatives, and a trailing service-volume modifier into one long sentence, making the main actions hard to follow."
  },
  {
    "find": "Passionate about reliability engineering and developer experience, Maya is a frequent contributor to open-source observability tooling and an advocate for pragmatic, incremental approaches to system evolution.",
    "replace": "Maya is passionate about reliability engineering and developer experience. She frequently contributes to open-source observability tooling and advocates pragmatic, incremental approaches to system evolution.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The introductory phrase delays the subject, and the sentence stacks two separate roles in one long statement; splitting it makes the point easier to say and read."
  }
]

[
  {
    "find": "Rust won't meaningfully change your p99.",
    "replace": "Rust won't change your p99 much.",
    "type": "empty-words",
    "reason": "'Meaningfully' is a vague intensifier; 'much' states the same hedge more plainly."
  },
  {
    "find": "**Rewrites carry substantial hidden costs:** feature freeze, re-establishing test coverage, team ramp-up on Rust's ownership model, and a long tail of behavioral differences that surface in production.",
    "replace": "**Rewrites carry substantial hidden costs.** These include a feature freeze, re-establishing test coverage, and getting the team up to speed on Rust's ownership model. There is also a long tail of behavioral differences that surface in production.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The 27-word sentence packs a long list and a relative clause into one sentence; splitting it makes each cost easy to parse."
  },
  {
    "find": "A well-isolated, CPU-heavy component (parsing, compression, numerical kernels) can be rewritten as a Rust extension via PyO3, delivering native performance without abandoning the Python codebase.",
    "replace": "If a component is well isolated and CPU-heavy (for example, parsing, compression, or numerical kernels), you can rewrite it as a Rust extension via PyO3. That gives you native performance without abandoning the Python codebase.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The passive, 25-word sentence and trailing participle make the reader hold the component, examples, and benefit until the end."
  },
  {
    "find": "If you're hitting memory limits or GC pauses that fundamentally constrain throughput.",
    "replace": "Rust also makes sense if you're hitting memory limits or GC pauses that fundamentally constrain throughput.",
    "type": "telegraphic",
    "reason": "As a bullet under 'When Rust does make sense,' this is a fragment with no main clause; adding the clause makes it a sentence."
  },
  {
    "find": "Ultimately, a hybrid approach — profile, optimize in Python, then surgically introduce Rust for proven hotspots — tends to offer the best risk-adjusted return.",
    "replace": "Ultimately, a hybrid approach tends to give the best result once you account for risk: profile, optimize in Python, then introduce Rust only for proven hotspots.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The dash aside sits between the subject and verb, so the reader has to hold the main point until the end."
  }
]

[
  {
    "find": "The platform's strengths — declarative orchestration, self-healing, horizontal scaling across heterogeneous workloads — address problems that typically emerge only once you have multiple services, meaningful traffic, and a dedicated infrastructure function.",
    "replace": "The platform's strengths are declarative orchestration, self-healing, and horizontal scaling across heterogeneous workloads. They address problems that typically emerge only once you have multiple services, traffic that is large enough to matter, and a dedicated infrastructure team.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The list of strengths is wedged between the subject and verb, and the sentence is long; stating the strengths first and then what they address is easier to follow."
  },
  {
    "find": "your scarcest resource is engineering time, and Kubernetes imposes a steep tax on it:",
    "replace": "engineering time is your scarcest resource. Kubernetes takes a large share of it.",
    "type": "jargon",
    "reason": "“Steep tax” is a business metaphor for time cost; saying Kubernetes takes a large share makes the cost plain."
  },
  {
    "find": "cluster upgrades, networking and ingress configuration, RBAC, observability setup, and a learning curve that pulls focus from product development.",
    "replace": "That work includes cluster upgrades, networking and ingress configuration, RBAC, and observability setup. A learning curve also pulls focus from product development.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The colon list runs long and changes from setup tasks to a learning curve; splitting gives each idea its own sentence."
  },
  {
    "find": "Managed PaaS offerings (Render, Fly.io, Railway) or a simple container service like AWS ECS or Cloud Run deliver containerized deployments, autoscaling, and zero-downtime releases with a fraction of the operational burden.",
    "replace": "Managed PaaS offerings (Render, Fly.io, Railway) or a simple container service like AWS ECS or Cloud Run give you containerized deployments, autoscaling, and zero-downtime releases. Using these services takes much less operational work.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The sentence is long and ends with an abstract comparison; splitting keeps the feature list and the lower operational cost separate."
  },
  {
    "find": "That said, there are legitimate exceptions: if your founding team has deep Kubernetes expertise, if you're building infrastructure tooling where Kubernetes is the product surface, or if enterprise customers require on-prem deployments via Helm charts, early adoption can be justified.",
    "replace": "That said, there are legitimate exceptions. Early adoption can be justified if your founding team has deep Kubernetes expertise. It can also be justified if you're building infrastructure tooling where Kubernetes is the product's main interface. And it can be justified if enterprise customers require on-prem deployments via Helm charts.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The three conditions are packed into one long sentence; separating them makes each exception easy to check."
  },
  {
    "find": "Otherwise, design for portability — containerize your services, keep configuration in environment variables — so that migrating later remains a straightforward exercise rather than a re-architecture.",
    "replace": "Otherwise, keep your design portable. Containerize your services and keep configuration in environment variables. Then migrating later will be straightforward and will not require a re-architecture.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "Dashes wedge a two-step instruction into a long sentence; splitting the steps from the result makes the advice easier to follow."
  }
]

[
  {
    "find": "As $\\lambda$ grows, variance decreases monotonically while bias increases, and the optimal $\\lambda$ — typically selected via cross-validation — minimizes total expected test error.",
    "replace": "As $\\lambda$ grows, variance decreases monotonically while bias increases. The optimal $\\lambda$ minimizes total expected test error; it is typically selected via cross-validation.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The parenthetical aside sits between the subject and its verb, so the reader has to hold the main claim across the dashes; splitting it puts the main claim first."
  },
  {
    "find": "There's also a Bayesian interpretation: L2 regularization is equivalent to MAP estimation under a Gaussian prior on the weights, while L1 (lasso) corresponds to a Laplace prior, which additionally induces sparsity by driving some weights exactly to zero.",
    "replace": "There is also a Bayesian interpretation: L2 regularization is equivalent to MAP estimation under a Gaussian prior on the weights. L1 (lasso) corresponds to a Laplace prior; this prior also induces sparsity by driving some weights exactly to zero.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The sentence is long and runs two comparisons plus a relative clause together; splitting it makes each claim easier to parse."
  },
  {
    "find": "In deep learning, techniques like dropout, weight decay, and early stopping serve analogous roles, each limiting the model's effective capacity to memorize idiosyncrasies of the training set.",
    "replace": "In deep learning, techniques like dropout, weight decay, and early stopping serve analogous roles. Each limits the model's effective capacity to memorize idiosyncrasies of the training set.",
    "type": "hard-clause",
    "reason": "The trailing participial phrase makes the sentence long and forces the reader to connect 'each' back to the list; a second sentence makes that link clear."
  }
]
